n{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186488899 1080 "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186488899 1080 "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186488917 1080 "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186488917 1080 "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186488925 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186488925 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186488938 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186488938 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186488977 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186488977 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186489173 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=3x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3x+2y \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+(3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 3k{a}_{1}+2k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186489173 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=3x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3x+2y \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+(3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 3k{a}_{1}+2k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186489232 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -10\\end{aligned}]