ic matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186490315 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186490324 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186490324 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186490324 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+8}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 3=5.\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186490324 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+8}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 3=5.\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186490328 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186490328 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186490328 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\