t curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{({y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-5 \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 3 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,4).$\n\n![](images/fe/fe39efb538d3270028fa5892402d56a8ae0e061968ecca8c676d8ad9f482644e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,{u}^{2}-5\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186491144 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{({y}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-5 \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 3 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,4).$\n\n![](images/fe/fe39efb538d3270028fa5892402d56a8ae0e061968ecca8c676d8ad9f482644e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,{u}^{2}-5\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186491154 1071 "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{