lpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186498584 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}.$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186498584 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}.$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186499449 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 false] [17592186499449 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac